  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Quadratic graphs"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are quadratic graphs?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The general equation of a quadratic graph is $y=ax^{2}+bx+c$\n- Their shape is called a parabola (\"U\" shape)\n- Positive quadratics have a value of  $a>0$ so the parabola is upright$\\cup$\n- Negative quadratics have a value of $a<0$ so the parabola is upside down $\\cap$"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/2.2.1-Quadratic-Graphs-Notes-Diagram-1.png",
   "file": "assets/6d7f5c08bdf4-2-2-1-quadratic-graphs-notes-diagram-1.png",
   "alt": "Quadratic Graphs Notes Diagram 1, A Level & AS Level Pure Maths Revision Notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I use quadratic graphs?"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "You need to be able to:"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **sketch** a quadratic graph given an equation or information about the graph\n- determine, from the equation, the **axes** **intercepts**\n- **factorise**, if possible, to find the *roots* of the quadratic function\n- find the coordinates of the ***turning point*** (maximum or minimum)"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "You may have to rearrange the equation before you can find some of these things"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/2.2.1-Quadratic-Graphs-Notes-Diagram-2.png",
   "file": "assets/0c0634c9fa61-2-2-1-quadratic-graphs-notes-diagram-2.png",
   "alt": "Quadratic Graphs Notes Diagram 2, A Level & AS Level Pure Maths Revision Notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Your calculator may tell you the **roots** of a quadratic function and the coordinates of the turning point\n- But don't rely on it – think about how many marks the question is worth and how much method/working you should show\n- Remember sometimes you'll need to rearrange an equation into the form $y=ax^{2}+bx+c$"
  },
